{"id":61,"course_id":16,"title":"Dockerfile Foundations and Runtime Contract","summary":"Write Dockerfiles that make the application runtime obvious, repeatable, and inspectable.","sequence":1,"body":"## Mental model\nA production Dockerfile is an operations document as much as a build recipe. Base image choice, working directory, copied files, user, exposed port, entrypoint, healthcheck, and build context all shape how the image behaves in CI, Kubernetes, and incidents.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice reading Dockerfiles for hidden assumptions: root user, broad COPY statements, unpinned package installs, secret leakage, missing CA certificates, and startup commands that differ from the app contract.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nReview one Dockerfile and produce a runtime contract: process, port, filesystem writes, required env vars, user, health endpoint, and how to collect logs.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","practice_notes":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-docker-image-supply-chain","course_category":"Docker","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":301,"term":"Docker Image and Supply Chain Operations","context":"Docker","definition":"Build production-ready container images with clear runtime contracts, multi-stage builds, immutable promotion, scanning, SBOMs, and safe registry habits."},{"id":302,"term":"Evidence","context":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":303,"term":"Blast radius","context":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":304,"term":"Rollback","context":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":305,"term":"Runbook","context":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":181,"lesson_id":61,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Dockerfile Foundations and Runtime Contract?","answer":"Write Dockerfiles that make the application runtime obvious, repeatable, and inspectable.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":182,"lesson_id":61,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":183,"lesson_id":61,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
